---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Base
  title="Statecraft: Israel Content System | Jeremy Hoover"
  description="How I built contributor guidance, reusable AI workflows, historical research, UX writing standards, and shipped interface copy for a publicly developed strategy game."
>
  <article class="section case-study case-study--violet">
    <header class="case-hero">
      <div class="container container--narrow">
        <a href={`${base}/#projects`} class="back"><span class="back-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Back to selected projects</span></a>

        <span class="tag"><span>Historical research</span><span>Game UX</span><span>Content systems</span></span>
        <h1>Designing the content system for a historical strategy game</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Drawing on my background in history and content design, I joined Statecraft: Israel as
          a public contributor. I conducted source-backed historical research and turned it into
          structured game content, then built reusable guidance, AI-assisted workflows,
          validation tools, and player-facing copy through 16 merged pull requests.
        </p>

        <div class="outcomes-strip" aria-label="Project outcomes">
          <div class="outcome">
            <span class="outcome__num">16 PRs</span>
            <span class="outcome__label">Public contributions merged into the project</span>
          </div>
          <div class="outcome">
            <span class="outcome__num">7 studies</span>
            <span class="outcome__label">Historical research and source-verification contributions</span>
          </div>
          <div class="outcome">
            <span class="outcome__num">4 skills</span>
            <span class="outcome__label">Reusable workflows for research, writing, UX, and video</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="case-body">
      <div class="container case-shell">
        <aside class="disclosure" aria-label="Project disclosure">
          <strong>My role</strong>
          <p>Statecraft: Israel was created by Eyal Golan. I did not create or own the game. This case study covers only my public issues and merged contributions. The repository uses different licenses for code, content, documentation, and assets.</p>
        </aside>

        <section class="case-summary" aria-labelledby="summary-title">
          <div class="reading-column">
            <h2 id="summary-title">At a glance</h2>
            <dl class="case-meta">
              <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Historical research, content systems, and UX-writing contributor</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Contribution</dt><dd>Identified gaps, proposed work publicly, and authored 16 merged pull requests</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Scope</dt><dd>Contributor guidance, AI skills, historical research, game data, UX copy, localization guidance, and release messaging</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Collaboration</dt><dd>Project owner, maintainers, and public contributors</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Timeframe</dt><dd>October 2026, ongoing public contribution</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Evidence</dt><dd>Public issues, pull requests, review history, tests, merged files, and contributor credit</dd></div>
            </dl>
          </div>
        </section>

        <nav class="case-toc" aria-label="Statecraft: Israel case study sections">
          <span class="case-toc__label">Jump to a section</span>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="#challenge">Challenge</a></li>
            <li><a href="#foundation">Foundation</a></li>
            <li><a href="#research">Research</a></li>
            <li><a href="#experience">Player experience</a></li>
            <li><a href="#evidence">Evidence</a></li>
            <li><a href="#reflection">Reflection</a></li>
          </ul>
        </nav>

        <section id="challenge" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>A game where every word carries evidence</h2>
          <p>
            Statecraft: Israel is a turn-based strategy game about governing the country from
            1948. It combines a deterministic simulation with real people, events, locations,
            and political decisions. Historical claims need sources, sensitive events require
            care, and interface text must explain unfamiliar systems without flattening the history.
          </p>
          <p>
            The repository was moving quickly across game design, Unity implementation,
            historical content, English and Hebrew localization, and public releases. I saw a
            content-design problem larger than any individual string: contributors and AI agents
            needed a shared way to research, write, validate, and communicate the game.
          </p>
          <p>
            My history background was directly relevant. I could move from archival and
            institutional sources to conflicting accounts, unresolved evidence, and the product
            decisions those findings supported. The work combined historical method with the
            implementation discipline of a public software repository.
          </p>
        </section>

        <figure class="gameplay-figure">
          <img src={`${base}/images/case-studies/statecraft-israel.jpg`} alt="Statecraft: Israel gameplay showing the 1948 map, city labels, resource indicators, and the Tel Aviv city-management panel" width="1600" height="900" />
          <figcaption>
            <strong>The playable game.</strong> This public repository screenshot shows the map,
            city systems, resources, and player guidance that the content system supports.
            Screenshot from the Statecraft: Israel repository, licensed under its documented asset terms.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <section id="foundation" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>Building the contributor foundation first</h2>
          <p>
            My first major contribution did not add a game event. It made the repository easier
            to contribute to. I added repository-level agent guidance, a writing guide,
            tool-specific instructions, reusable skills, templates, and validation scripts.
          </p>
          <p>
            That decision made later work repeatable. Instead of solving source handling, voice,
            or implementation context again in every pull request, contributors and AI agents
            could start from shared rules and follow the authoritative project sources.
          </p>
        </section>

        <figure class="system-figure" aria-labelledby="system-caption">
          <div class="system-grid" role="img" aria-label="Four layers of the contribution system: repository guidance, reusable skills, validation, and product delivery">
            <div><span>01</span><strong>Guidance</strong><small>Repository rules, writing standards, and source authority</small></div>
            <div><span>02</span><strong>Skills</strong><small>Historical content, writing voice, UX writing, and video scripts</small></div>
            <div><span>03</span><strong>Validation</strong><small>Templates, checkers, tests, and explicit uncertainty</small></div>
            <div><span>04</span><strong>Delivery</strong><small>Research, structured data, interface copy, and release messaging</small></div>
          </div>
          <figcaption id="system-caption">
            <strong>Standards connected to delivery.</strong> The system joined repository guidance
            to reusable workflows and then applied both to player-facing work.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <section id="research" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>Turning research into product infrastructure</h2>
          <p>
            I contributed public research on the New Economic Policy, the Knesset vote on talks
            with Germany, the 1955 election, the 1948 war, the road to Suez, and the Sinai
            Campaign. I also verified the sources behind attributed trailer quotations.
          </p>
          <p>
            The useful output was not just a collection of facts. The notes distinguish verified
            claims, secondary leads, conflicts between sources, and unresolved gaps. That makes
            uncertainty visible before material becomes structured content or player-facing copy.
          </p>
          <p>
            I then carried the research into the product by adding structured Third Knesset and
            1955 election data, including source references and tests that verified the new
            records fit the simulation's content model.
          </p>
        </section>

        <figure class="system-figure" aria-labelledby="pipeline-caption">
          <ol class="research-flow">
            <li><span>1</span><strong>Question</strong><small>Define the historical or product gap</small></li>
            <li><span>2</span><strong>Sources</strong><small>Open, compare, and record evidence</small></li>
            <li><span>3</span><strong>Research note</strong><small>Separate facts, conflicts, and gaps</small></li>
            <li><span>4</span><strong>Game content</strong><small>Structure reviewed material for the system</small></li>
            <li><span>5</span><strong>Player experience</strong><small>Explain decisions and consequences clearly</small></li>
          </ol>
          <figcaption id="pipeline-caption">
            <strong>A source-first pipeline.</strong> Historical material moves through explicit
            evidence states before it reaches the game experience.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <section id="experience" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>Making complex systems understandable to players</h2>
          <p>
            I audited the implemented interface before proposing new standards. That work became
            a 49-concept glossary, six shared conventions, and English and Hebrew UI-writing
            guidance tied to the actual game rather than generic style advice.
          </p>
          <p>
            I applied the guidance in shipped UI changes. The revised save warnings explain what
            changed and what a player should do next. The How to play content defines unfamiliar
            training terms, connects facilities to outcomes, and makes clear when worker changes
            take effect. Draft Hebrew remained marked for native review rather than being
            presented as final.
          </p>
          <p>
            I also built reusable UX-writing and sourced video-script skills with templates and
            checks. The goal was to make good decisions easier to repeat, while still leaving
            source review, native-language review, and maintainer approval visible.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="evidence" class="content-section">
          <div class="reading-column">
            <h2>Public, inspectable evidence</h2>
            <p>
              Every contribution is visible in the repository, including the original proposal,
              review discussion, revisions, tests, and merge. The record shows both the reusable
              system and the product work it enabled.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="proof-list" aria-label="Public contribution evidence">
            <a href="https://github.com/eyalgolan/statecraft-israel/pulls?q=is%3Apr+author%3Ajeremydhoover-blip+is%3Amerged" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Complete record</span><strong>Review all 16 merged pull requests</strong><small>Research, systems, content, UX, and messaging ↗</small></a>
            <a href="https://github.com/eyalgolan/statecraft-israel/pull/134" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Foundation</span><strong>Inspect the repository guidance and skills</strong><small>Writing guide, agent instructions, templates, and checks ↗</small></a>
            <a href="https://github.com/eyalgolan/statecraft-israel/pull/970" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>UX system</span><strong>Read the bilingual UX guidance contribution</strong><small>Glossary, conventions, English, and Hebrew guidance ↗</small></a>
            <a href="https://github.com/eyalgolan/statecraft-israel/pull/1139" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Shipped UI copy</span><strong>Compare the revised save and recovery messages</strong><small>Implementation evidence, review, and before-and-after captures ↗</small></a>
            <a href="https://github.com/eyalgolan/statecraft-israel/pull/1140" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Player guidance</span><strong>Inspect the revised How to play content</strong><small>Desktop and mobile evidence in English and Hebrew ↗</small></a>
            <a href="https://github.com/eyalgolan/statecraft-israel/blob/main/site/src/data/contributors.yaml" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Contributor credit</span><strong>View the project's public contributor record</strong><small>Repository-maintained attribution ↗</small></a>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="reflection" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>
          <p>
            This work shows what content design can look like in a public, AI-assisted software
            project. The contribution was not limited to writing interface strings. It connected
            source authority, contributor behavior, agent instructions, validation, structured
            data, localization, and release communication.
          </p>
          <p>
            The public review process also made the boundaries clear. Maintainers owned the merge
            decision, native speakers owned final language review, and unresolved historical
            evidence stayed unresolved. The system helped AI accelerate the work without hiding
            where human judgment and further review were still required.
          </p>
        </section>

        <aside class="inspect-work" aria-labelledby="inspect-title">
          <div>
            <span class="tag">Public contribution</span>
            <h2 id="inspect-title">Inspect the work and the game</h2>
            <p>The live repository contains the contribution history, current game, source policies, tests, and project roadmap.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="inspect-work__actions">
            <a href="https://github.com/eyalgolan/statecraft-israel/pulls?q=is%3Apr+author%3Ajeremydhoover-blip+is%3Amerged" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn">View my pull requests ↗</a>
            <a href="https://github.com/eyalgolan/statecraft-israel" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn--outline">Explore the repository ↗</a>
          </div>
        </aside>

        <hr class="divider" />
        <nav class="case-pagination" aria-label="Case study navigation">
          <a href={`${base}/work/fabric-cli`} class="case-pagination__link">
            <span class="case-pagination__icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="case-pagination__content"><span class="case-pagination__direction">Previous project</span><span class="case-pagination__title">Fabric CLI AI skills</span></span>
          </a>
          <a href={`${base}/#projects`} class="case-pagination__link">
            <span class="case-pagination__icon case-pagination__icon--index" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="case-pagination__content"><span class="case-pagination__direction">Project index</span><span class="case-pagination__title">All selected projects</span></span>
          </a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .case-study { padding-top: 0; }
  .tag { display: inline-block; margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  h1 { margin-block: var(--space-4) var(--space-6); }
  .lede { max-width: 58ch; margin-bottom: var(--space-12); font-size: var(--size-xl); line-height: 1.6; }
  .outcome { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-2); transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .outcome:hover { transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .outcome__num { line-height: 1.1; white-space: nowrap; }
  .outcome__label { max-width: 24ch; color: var(--muted); font-size: var(--size-sm); line-height: 1.5; }
  .case-shell { max-width: calc(var(--narrow) + 6rem); }
  .reading-column { width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); margin-inline: auto; }
  .content-section { scroll-margin-top: 90px; }
  .content-section h2, .case-summary h2 { margin-bottom: var(--space-6); }
  .content-section:not(:first-child) h2 { margin-top: var(--space-16); }
  .content-section p { margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }

  .disclosure { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 120px 1fr; gap: var(--space-6); width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); margin: 0 auto var(--space-12); padding: var(--space-6); border: 1px solid var(--portfolio-blue-soft); border-left: 4px solid var(--portfolio-blue); border-radius: 12px; background: #fff; }
  .disclosure strong { color: var(--portfolio-blue); font-size: 0.75rem; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .disclosure p { color: var(--muted); font-size: var(--size-sm); }

  .case-meta { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--space-3); }
  .case-meta div { padding: var(--space-6); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-left: 4px solid var(--portfolio-blue-soft); border-radius: 12px; background: #fff; transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .case-meta div:hover { transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .case-meta dt { margin-bottom: var(--space-2); color: var(--portfolio-blue); font-size: 0.75rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .case-meta dd { color: var(--portfolio-ink); font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.5; }

  .case-toc { max-width: var(--narrow); margin: var(--space-8) auto var(--space-16); padding-block: var(--space-6); border-block: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .case-toc__label { display: block; margin-bottom: var(--space-3); color: var(--portfolio-blue); font-size: 0.6875rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .case-toc ul { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.5rem 1.5rem; margin: 0; list-style: none; }
  .case-toc a { display: inline-flex; min-height: 32px; align-items: center; color: var(--portfolio-ink); font-size: 0.875rem; font-weight: 650; text-decoration: none; }
  .case-toc a:hover { color: var(--portfolio-blue); text-decoration: underline; text-decoration-color: var(--portfolio-coral); text-decoration-thickness: 2px; text-underline-offset: 5px; }

  .gameplay-figure { width: min(100%, 880px); margin: var(--space-12) auto var(--space-16); }
  .system-figure { width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); margin: var(--space-12) auto var(--space-16); }
  .gameplay-figure img { width: 100%; aspect-ratio: 16 / 9; object-fit: cover; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 18px; box-shadow: 0 14px 34px rgba(16,26,45,0.12); }
  .gameplay-figure figcaption, .system-figure figcaption { margin-top: var(--space-4); color: var(--muted); font-size: var(--size-sm); line-height: 1.55; }
  .gameplay-figure figcaption strong, .system-figure figcaption strong { color: var(--portfolio-ink); }

  .system-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--space-3); padding: var(--space-4); border: 1px solid #b8c9ee; border-radius: 20px; background: var(--portfolio-blue-soft); }
  .system-grid div { display: flex; min-height: 145px; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-2); padding: var(--space-6); border-radius: 12px; background: #fff; transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .system-grid div:hover { transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .system-grid span, .research-flow > li > span { display: grid; width: 1.8rem; height: 1.8rem; place-items: center; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--portfolio-blue); color: #fff; font-size: 0.7rem; font-weight: 800; }
  .system-grid strong, .research-flow strong { color: var(--portfolio-ink); }
  .system-grid small, .research-flow small { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.8rem; line-height: 1.5; }

  .research-flow { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(5, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--space-2); margin: 0; padding: var(--space-4); border: 1px solid #efc4b8; border-radius: 20px; background: #ffe8e2; list-style: none; }
  .research-flow li { position: relative; display: flex; min-height: 165px; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-2); padding: var(--space-4); border-radius: 12px; background: #fff; }

  .proof-list { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); gap: var(--space-3); margin: var(--space-8) auto 0; }
  .proof-list a { display: flex; min-height: 150px; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-2); padding: var(--space-6); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-top: 6px solid var(--portfolio-blue); border-radius: 12px; background: #fff; color: var(--portfolio-ink); text-decoration: none; transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .proof-list a:nth-child(2n) { border-top-color: var(--portfolio-coral); }
  .proof-list a:hover { transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 12px 28px rgba(16,26,45,0.12); }
  .proof-list span { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.7rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .proof-list strong { color: var(--portfolio-blue); line-height: 1.4; }
  .proof-list small { margin-top: auto; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.78rem; line-height: 1.45; }

  .inspect-work { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr auto; gap: var(--space-8); align-items: center; width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); margin: var(--space-16) auto 0; padding: clamp(1.5rem, 5vw, 3rem); border-radius: 20px; background: var(--portfolio-blue); }
  .inspect-work .tag { color: var(--portfolio-yellow); }
  .inspect-work h2 { margin-bottom: var(--space-3); color: #fff; }
  .inspect-work p { color: rgba(255,255,255,0.82); }
  .inspect-work__actions { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-3); min-width: 215px; }
  .inspect-work .btn { justify-content: center; text-align: center; }
  .inspect-work .btn--outline { color: #fff; border-color: var(--portfolio-yellow); }
  .divider { margin-block: var(--space-16) var(--space-8); }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .research-flow { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
    .research-flow li { min-height: 140px; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .content-section { scroll-margin-top: 130px; }
    .disclosure, .case-meta, .system-grid, .proof-list, .inspect-work { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .disclosure { gap: var(--space-2); padding: var(--space-4); }
    .case-meta div { padding: var(--space-4); }
    .case-toc { margin-block: var(--space-6) var(--space-12); padding-block: var(--space-4); }
    .case-toc ul { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--space-1) var(--space-6); }
    .case-toc a { min-height: 44px; }
    .research-flow { grid-template-columns: 1fr; padding: var(--space-3); }
    .research-flow li, .system-grid div, .proof-list a { min-height: auto; }
    .inspect-work__actions { min-width: 0; }
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .outcome:hover, .case-meta div:hover, .system-grid div:hover, .proof-list a:hover { transform: none; }
  }
</style>
